00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:04.249 align:start position:0%
exactly a hundred years ago the map of  the world was redrawn the biggest  conflict so far known to mankind the  first world war finally came to an end  in November 1918 the changes that  followed were perhaps more strongly felt  in the Middle East than in any other  region of the world especially due to  the collapse of the Ottoman Empire this  is the story of the First World War and  the impact that it is still having today  told from the perspective of Turkey and  the Arab world the first world war saw  the collapse of four great powers the  German and Russian empires and  austro-hungary the ultimate Empire was  also on the losing side its territories  were divided up by the victors they  promised the Turkish nationalists their  own state but Greece and France claimed  parts of the country with the biggest  city Istanbul controlled by the US and  the British the Turks were enraged a  young officer named Mustafa Kemal fought  successfully with the remnants of the  Ottoman army against Greek and Italian  forces the war hero later began calling  himself Ataturk the father of the Turks  he founded a modern nation-state  imposing disciplined authoritarian rule  Ataturk maintained a strict division  between the secular state and Islam the  country's dominant religion Turkey  remains to this day an independent  nation the bridge between east and west  the Arab people's in contrast were  unable to secure their independence  freed from the subjugation of the  Ottomans they found themselves instead  under British and French rule once it  became apparent that the Ottoman Empire  could not survive the war the British  and French organised clandestine talks  under a cloak of secrecy they decided  how to divide up the Middle East the  British claimed the city of Mosul and  its oil they also wanted Jerusalem as a  base to control the new state of  Palestine France meanwhile allowed  Britain a free hand in taking over  territory in return for demanding huge  war  operations from Germany both the French  and British initially pledged to  transform their Arab territories into  independent democracies which never came  about  instead resources and goodwill were  exploited  the legacy of the First World War still  weighs heavily on the Middle East  arbitrary borders and lack of democratic  structures profound mistrust of the West  or as one American historian put it the  end of the first world war in Middle  East was a peace to end all peace
 

